WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.457
Secrets management is tested as much in the development workflow as in production.

2
00:00:05.457 --> 00:00:11.772
Local development often uses .env-like files, shell variables, or local configuration files.

3
00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:22.687
That can be fine for developer convenience, but those files must stay out of source control, use development-only values, and avoid sharing production credentials.

4
00:00:22.687 --> 00:00:31.340
A local secret should not unlock production simply because the code path is the same. Go tests are a good place to enforce boundaries.

5
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:40.228
Instead of calling a real managed secret store in ordinary unit tests, define a small provider interface and use a fake implementation.

6
00:00:40.228 --> 00:00:47.712
The fake returns known nonsecret values or controlled errors. This makes tests faster, more reliable, and safer.

7
00:00:47.712 --> 00:00:56.210
It also lets you test fail-closed behavior: missing secret, empty value, provider timeout, revoked value, and refresh failure.

8
00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:06.423
Integration tests can exercise a real provider when needed, but they should run in a controlled environment with dedicated test secrets and clear cleanup.

9
00:01:06.423 --> 00:01:13.361
Avoid realistic secrets in fixtures. A token that looks real can trigger scanners and confuse reviewers.

10
00:01:13.361 --> 00:01:17.649
Worse, a copied production header in a fixture may actually work.

11
00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:24.588
Use obvious nonworking values such as test-only-token or example.invalid when a placeholder is enough.

12
00:01:24.588 --> 00:01:29.733
If a test proves redaction, assert that sensitive input is absent from output.

13
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:38.933
If a config loader fails, assert that the error names the missing key without including the secret value. CI and CD systems need special care.

14
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:47.041
Pipeline variables, logs, artifacts, cache directories, test reports, and container build output may all expose values.

15
00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:56.240
Treat pipeline output as sensitive. Disable shell tracing around secret-handling steps. Avoid commands that print entire environments.

16
00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.216
Do not bake secrets into Docker images or compiled binaries.

17
00:02:00.216 --> 00:02:09.416
If a build needs access to a secret, prefer a scoped CI secret or workload identity that is available only to the required job and environment.

18
00:02:09.416 --> 00:02:13.470
Secret scanning is a useful control, not a perfect guarantee.

19
00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:20.876
Scanners can catch common key formats, high-entropy strings, and known credential patterns before they merge.

20
00:02:20.876 --> 00:02:26.099
They should run in pre-commit hooks, pull requests, or CI gates where possible.

21
00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:35.299
But scanning does not replace code review, redaction, or rotation planning. If a real secret is committed, treat it as exposed and rotate it.

22
00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:42.861
Finally, keep environments separate. Development, test, staging, and production should not share credentials.

23
00:02:42.861 --> 00:02:50.891
A staging bug should not expose production access. A developer laptop should not need production secrets for normal work.

24
00:02:50.891 --> 00:03:04.534
The safer workflow is boring: fake providers in unit tests, dedicated test credentials for integration tests, scoped CI secrets, scanning gates, and production access only where the service truly needs it.

25
00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:08.432
Local development should also teach the production habit.

26
00:03:08.432 --> 00:03:18.021
If developers routinely run the service with explicit nonworking values, fake providers, and ignored local files, the safe path becomes normal.

27
00:03:18.021 --> 00:03:24.648
If the easiest path requires copying production secrets, the workflow is training people into risk.

28
00:03:24.648 --> 00:03:29.170
Good tooling makes the secure path the fast path for everyday Go work.
